Send cdp's ASA back to the peer the ASR came from, once.

cdp answers an ASR itself, from the session state machine, before the
request handlers run. That ASA was built with AAANewMessage(IMS_ASA, 0, ...),
so it had Application-Id 0 instead of the request's, and:
- with no session it said 2001 and went out through AAASendMessage(), which
  routes on the Application-Id AVPs an ASA does not have, so it was never
  sent and the peer timed out;
- with a session it went to the peer named by Origin-Host, which is not the
  one it came from when the ASR crossed a relay.
RFC 6733 6.2: an answer carries the request's Application-Id and goes back
to the peer the request came from; 8.5.2: an ASR for a session that is not
active is answered with 5002 DIAMETER_UNKNOWN_SESSION_ID.

Build the ASA with AAACreateResponse(), answer 5002 when there is no
session, and send it to the peer the ASR came from (now recorded in the
message's in_peer), Origin-Host only as a fallback.

Mark the request as answered, so a request handler's answer to it is
dropped instead of being sent as a second answer (ims_charging also answers
a Ro ASR that cdp answered for lack of a session), and the no-answer
fallback skips it. That replaces the "ASR with a Session-Id" exception of
the fallback: an ASR the state machine did not answer, in a state where it
does not, now gets 5012 instead of nothing. Only one answer goes out when
several handlers return one.

